{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794137235,"nonce":"D5dsvZsGFqpl7fs3kesxsLNCzX0=","title":"Native Metal backend for macOS and iOS behind the render backend interface","message":"After the narrow interface exists, implement Metal directly for Apple targets and leave wgpu for the rest, as GPUI does. Metal is the smallest backend to write, and macOS and iOS are where the wgpu stack costs the most compile time (ash and the Vulkan backend are compiled on macOS only because the desktop preset asks for vulkan-portability; naga 12.5 s, wgpu-core 8.8 s, wgpu-hal 4.1 s in a clean build). It would also cut memory: a reviewer measured an empty wgpu window at about 100 MB against about 10 MB for Zed's custom renderer (second-hand, unverified).\n\nNeeds the precompiled MSL from the offline shader issue. Decide the Metal bindings crate (objc2-metal compiles slowly; weigh it against the metal crate wgpu uses today) and whether to require Metal 4 or target an older feature set. Keep wgpu as the fallback and as the only path on web, Android, Linux and Windows until a native backend there is justified.\n\nParent: the lean GPU stack plan.","files":null}]}